\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S16}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What would acting on CKA have saved?} The same 31 cells read as a decision rather than a correlation. A practitioner choosing per cell between full fine-tuning and a frozen encoder collects $\Delta _\text  {enc}$ (positive ${=}$ freezing better) on every cell they freeze, so \emph  {saving} $=$ mean over all 31 cells of $\Delta _\text  {enc}\cdot \mathbf  {1}[\text  {policy freezes}]$, in percentage points of MSE, against the default of always adapting. \textbf  {The out-of-sample row is the only one a practitioner could have followed}: the threshold and its orientation are searched on the 14 training clusters and applied unchanged to the held-out cluster, so each cell's decision is made without seeing it. The row below it is the same search run on all cells at once, and the difference between them, $+1.10$~pp $[0, +13]$, prices the optimism in a retrospectively chosen cut. \textbf  {The headline is the interval, not the point estimate}: the best-orientation rule's $+7.47$~pp comes with $[-4, +16]$ and is ${\leq }0$ in 25\% of draws, so it is a policy whose value these cells cannot establish, not one they refute. \textbf  {And the orientation that earns it is the inverse of the prescribed one}: freezing where drift is \emph  {largest} -- what forgetting mitigation actually recommends -- is worth $-6.97$~pp $[-10, +14]$, and the row above it buys its point estimate by freezing the cells that moved \emph  {least}, a direction available only because we searched both. That rule still puts 8 of the 31 cells on the wrong side of its own decision out of sample (16 of them wanted freezing), against 5 for the in-sample cut. \textbf  {This analysis is post-hoc and descriptive}: unlike Table~\ref {tab:cka_loco}, nothing here was pre-registered, and the family searched is a single global CKA threshold in either orientation -- a per-backbone or multivariate rule could do better, and 15 clusters cannot rule that out. Candidate cuts are interior (midpoints between consecutive distinct CKA values), so the search cannot return \emph  {always freeze} or \emph  {always adapt} and have it counted as CKA informing the decision; both are priced as rows of their own. Intervals are 95\% percentile cluster bootstrap (10,000/10,000 draws kept, a draw discarded when it leaves fewer than three distinct clusters), re-running the whole leave-one-cluster-out procedure inside each draw with duplicated clusters sharing one fold id. Every statistic is a mean of bounded per-cell quantities, so unlike $\Delta R^2$ in Table~\ref {tab:cka_loco} these percentiles are reproducible -- \textbf  {to whole percentage points, which is why they are quoted that way}: at two decimals the bounds move across bootstrap seeds (this table's headline lower bound came out $-3.97$, $-3.61$, $-3.68$ and $-3.67$ on seeds 0, 1, 7 and 13), and \texttt  {-{}-seedcheck} checks every figure quoted here at its printed precision. The point estimates and the misplacement counts carry no seed at all.}}{21}{table.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cka_decision}{{S16}{21}{\textbf {What would acting on CKA have saved?} The same 31 cells read as a decision rather than a correlation. A practitioner choosing per cell between full fine-tuning and a frozen encoder collects $\denc $ (positive ${=}$ freezing better) on every cell they freeze, so \emph {saving} $=$ mean over all 31 cells of $\denc \cdot \mathbf {1}[\text {policy freezes}]$, in percentage points of MSE, against the default of always adapting. \textbf {The out-of-sample row is the only one a practitioner could have followed}: the threshold and its orientation are searched on the 14 training clusters and applied unchanged to the held-out cluster, so each cell's decision is made without seeing it. The row below it is the same search run on all cells at once, and the difference between them, $+1.10$~pp $[0, +13]$, prices the optimism in a retrospectively chosen cut. \textbf {The headline is the interval, not the point estimate}: the best-orientation rule's $+7.47$~pp comes with $[-4, +16]$ and is ${\leq }0$ in 25\% of draws, so it is a policy whose value these cells cannot establish, not one they refute. \textbf {And the orientation that earns it is the inverse of the prescribed one}: freezing where drift is \emph {largest} -- what forgetting mitigation actually recommends -- is worth $-6.97$~pp $[-10, +14]$, and the row above it buys its point estimate by freezing the cells that moved \emph {least}, a direction available only because we searched both. That rule still puts 8 of the 31 cells on the wrong side of its own decision out of sample (16 of them wanted freezing), against 5 for the in-sample cut. \textbf {This analysis is post-hoc and descriptive}: unlike Table~\ref {tab:cka_loco}, nothing here was pre-registered, and the family searched is a single global CKA threshold in either orientation -- a per-backbone or multivariate rule could do better, and 15 clusters cannot rule that out. Candidate cuts are interior (midpoints between consecutive distinct CKA values), so the search cannot return \emph {always freeze} or \emph {always adapt} and have it counted as CKA informing the decision; both are priced as rows of their own. Intervals are 95\% percentile cluster bootstrap (10,000/10,000 draws kept, a draw discarded when it leaves fewer than three distinct clusters), re-running the whole leave-one-cluster-out procedure inside each draw with duplicated clusters sharing one fold id. Every statistic is a mean of bounded per-cell quantities, so unlike $\Delta R^2$ in Table~\ref {tab:cka_loco} these percentiles are reproducible -- \textbf {to whole percentage points, which is why they are quoted that way}: at two decimals the bounds move across bootstrap seeds (this table's headline lower bound came out $-3.97$, $-3.61$, $-3.68$ and $-3.67$ on seeds 0, 1, 7 and 13), and \texttt {-{}-seedcheck} checks every figure quoted here at its printed precision. The point estimates and the misplacement counts carry no seed at all}{table.caption.18}{}}
